WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:05.848
There are only two states in the United States of America that are not connected to the others.

2
00:00:05.849 --> 00:00:09.521
One is Hawaii, and the other is. . .Alaska!

3
00:00:09.522 --> 00:00:13.195
Alaska is also the largest state in the United States.

4
00:00:13.196 --> 00:00:23.777
It has glaciers and marshland, over 3.5 million lakes, and its capital cannot be reached by car─you have to take a boat or an airplane to get to Juneau!

5
00:00:23.778 --> 00:00:30.824
During the summers the sun shines on Alaska even at night, due to its location in the far North.

6
00:00:30.825 --> 00:00:42.114
Even though Alaska has these wonderful attractions, it is one of the least populated states in America, perhaps because of the extreme weather it experiences during the winters.

7
00:00:42.115 --> 00:00:48.383
Many people aren't aware of Alaska's natural wonders nor its fascinating history.

8
00:00:48.384 --> 00:00:55.653
Most scientists believe that people first arrived in America by walking over the Bering Strait and through Alaska.

9
00:00:55.654 --> 00:01:00.923
You might say, "Wait, that's impossible! The Bering Strait is water!"

10
00:01:00.924 --> 00:01:09.858
But during several ice ages, a bridge of ice over 1,600 kilometers long formed between Siberia and Alaska.

11
00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:18.005
The native Alaskans─Inuit, Aleuts, and other groups─crossed this bridge long before Europeans set foot in America.

12
00:01:18.006 --> 00:01:25.672
The Bering Strait is named after one of the first Europeans to travel in the area, a Russian named Vitus Bering.

13
00:01:25.673 --> 00:01:31.400
In 1741 he was commissioned by the Russian navy to survey the region.

14
00:01:31.401 --> 00:01:39.782
Soon after the arrival of Bering and his crew, others came to take advantage of the natural resources available in the area.

15
00:01:39.782 --> 00:01:50.038
Demand for sea otter pelts led hunters and traders to establish a fur trade on an archipelago off the west coast of Alaska, called the Aleutian Islands.

16
00:01:50.039 --> 00:01:59.834
The hunters were so greedy that they killed nearly all of the islands' furbearing creatures as they made their way through the Aleutian Islands toward mainland Alaska.

17
00:01:59.835 --> 00:02:06.245
They also enslaved much of the native population and forced them to hunt animals for the traders.

18
00:02:06.246 --> 00:02:18.846
In 1784 Grigori Shelekhov, a Russian merchant, and two ships full of hunters and traders arrived to set up the first non-native settlement in Alaska, called Russian America.

19
00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:29.898
Shelekhov then founded a trading company called the Russian-American Company and situated its headquarters in another settlement called Sitka in 1804.

20
00:02:29.899 --> 00:02:37.000
One of the company's managers started the first conservation program in North America for the endangered sea otter.

21
00:02:37.001 --> 00:02:45.056
This program, however, led to less money for Sitka because it limited the number of sea otter pelts hunters could collect.

22
00:02:45.057 --> 00:02:56.322
The people of Russian America tried to develop other industries, such as coal mining and whaling; unfortunately none of them generated enough money to stay in business.

23
00:02:56.323 --> 00:03:04.052
Then, when Russia lost the Crimean War in 1856, the czar decided to sell Russian America.

24
00:03:04.053 --> 00:03:15.756
The United States Secretary of State, William H. Seward, wanted to secure the land for the U.S. and agreed to buy Russian America for $7,200,000.

25
00:03:15.757 --> 00:03:24.789
Congress approved the purchase, and the Treaty of Cession of Russian America to the United States was signed on March 30, 1867.

26
00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:29.343
This legally placed the land under the control of the United States.

27
00:03:29.344 --> 00:03:35.333
Many Americans, however, believed that this was a costly and unnecessary acquisition.

28
00:03:35.334 --> 00:03:43.007
Unhappy with the decision, the public began referring to Alaska as "Seward's Folly" and "Seward's Icebox."

29
00:03:43.008 --> 00:03:51.400
Nevertheless American troops watched as the flag was raised at Sitka on October 18, 1867.

30
00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:57.627
After Alaska joined the union, some American companies tried to establish businesses there.

31
00:03:57.628 --> 00:04:02.998
For example, canneries were set up to support Alaska's new salmon fisheries.

32
00:04:02.999 --> 00:04:16.647
In addition Western Union, a communications company, attempted to lay a telegraph line from America all the way to Moscow, Russia, by going over the Bering Strait and through Siberia in 1866.

33
00:04:16.648 --> 00:04:29.050
Unfortunately they failed to get the telegraph line past the Yukon, a Canadian territory neighboring Alaska on the east, but their expedition inspired many others to explore Alaska.

34
00:04:29.051 --> 00:04:37.703
The discovery of gold in the Klondike region of the Yukon in 1896 also fueled Americans' curiosity about Alaska.

35
00:04:37.704 --> 00:04:48.197
Fortune-seekers surged toward the Yukon during the Klondike Gold Rush, one of the most well-known in history, and the fastest way to the Yukon was through Alaska.

36
00:04:48.198 --> 00:04:52.074
Alaska's population nearly doubled in the following decade.

37
00:04:52.075 --> 00:04:57.700
This rise in population forced the U.S. Congress to take note of Alaska.

38
00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:03.736
In 1912 Congress allowed Alaskans to legislate certain territorial issues.

39
00:05:03.737 --> 00:05:11.673
With this new power, Alaskan natives created the Alaska Native Brotherhood in 1913 to unite their communities.

40
00:05:11.674 --> 00:05:18.634
They petitioned for political and voting rights, integrated classrooms, and other civil rights for native Alaskans.

41
00:05:18.635 --> 00:05:27.801
Despite the economic progress made in the first decade of the twentieth century, Alaska suffered through the Great Depression with the rest of the United States.

42
00:05:27.802 --> 00:05:33.601
Two of Alaska's most vital resources, fish and copper, declined in value.

43
00:05:33.602 --> 00:05:40.395
Storeowners were making less money, so they either had to lower their employees' wages or fire them.

44
00:05:40.396 --> 00:05:46.321
More than half of Alaska's workforce lost their jobs during the Great Depression.

45
00:05:46.321 --> 00:05:51.261
When World War II began, Alaska's employment rate skyrocketed.

46
00:05:51.262 --> 00:06:00.811
Over 150,000 military personnel were stationed in newly built military installations in Alaska due to its proximity to Japan.

47
00:06:00.812 --> 00:06:06.190
The U.S. government also built the Alaska Highway as a military supply road.

48
00:06:06.191 --> 00:06:14.372
Recognizing Alaska's military importance, the Japanese bombed and occupied the Aleutian Islands in June of 1942.

49
00:06:14.373 --> 00:06:20.406
This was the only part of North America to be invaded and occupied during the Second World War.

50
00:06:20.406 --> 00:06:30.050
Alaska had already applied for statehood once, but it wasn't granted statehood until after U.S. officials saw how strategic its location truly was.

51
00:06:30.051 --> 00:06:47.528
So with the cold war looming, and Alaska being the closest U.S. territory to the Soviet Union, President Dwight D. Eisenhower signed the law that allowed Alaska to become the 49th state of the United States of America on January 3, 1959.

52
00:06:47.529 --> 00:06:51.153
Alaska's good fortune did not last long, however.

53
00:06:51.154 --> 00:06:59.406
On March 27, 1964, the newest U.S. state was hit by one of the most powerful earthquakes ever recorded.

54
00:06:59.407 --> 00:07:10.061
The Good Friday Earthquake, as it was called, killed 131 people and created mudslides and tsunamis that destroyed the port towns of Valdez and Cordova.

55
00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:14.408
Both cities were rebuilt on higher ground after the earthquake.

56
00:07:14.409 --> 00:07:21.510
Valdez also suffered another disaster when the Exxon Valdez, an oil tanker, crashed on a nearby reef.

57
00:07:21.511 --> 00:07:32.512
Oil spilled over nearly two thousand kilometers of the coastline and affected much of the area's wildlife, particularly salmon, sea otters, seals, and sea birds.

58
00:07:32.513 --> 00:07:40.005
The oil industry has been a cause for concern in Alaska since oil was discovered in the state in 1968.

59
00:07:40.006 --> 00:07:52.875
Alaskans managed to construct the Trans-Alaska Pipeline System, which transports oil from Prudhoe Bay over eight hundred miles to the rebuilt city of Valdez, without disturbing the land or wildlife surrounding it.

60
00:07:52.876 --> 00:08:03.761
Unfortunately the large oil reserves that were found beneath the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge, a protected area of Alaska, cannot be accessed without drilling.

61
00:08:03.762 --> 00:08:09.347
This disruption would negatively affect many of the species that are living in the preserve.

62
00:08:09.348 --> 00:08:16.076
Since the late 1970s, no one has been able to come up with a solution to this problem.

63
00:08:16.077 --> 00:08:20.850
Alaskans are still struggling to coexist peacefully with their environment.

64
00:08:20.851 --> 00:08:28.460
Most want to utilize their natural resources to further develop their state and improve the lives of its residents.

65
00:08:28.461 --> 00:08:32.441
Others think that the natural beauty of Alaska should be preserved.

66
00:08:32.442 --> 00:08:40.171
These people think that Alaska's wildlife and awe-inspiring scenery will attract tourists and ensure its economic future.

67
00:08:40.172 --> 00:08:48.914
The decisions today's leaders make will shape the future of Alaska, just as past leaders have made Alaska what it is today.

